--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -20,6 +20,21 @@ A file that cannot be read is reported on stderr as `tally: cannot read <path>`,
 the other files are still counted, and the exit code is 1. An unknown option
 prints a usage line on stderr and exits with code 2. Otherwise the exit code is 0.
 
+With `--json`, which may appear anywhere among the arguments, stdout gets a
+single JSON document followed by a newline instead of the text table:
+
+```
+node bin/tally.js --json notes.txt todo.txt
+```
+
+```json
+{"files":[{"path":"notes.txt","lines":3,"words":12,"chars":80},{"path":"todo.txt","lines":1,"words":4,"chars":19}],"total":{"lines":4,"words":16,"chars":99}}
+```
+
+`files` has one entry per file that was read, in command-line order. `total`
+is always present, even for a single file or none, and unreadable files are
+handled as in text mode: reported on stderr, left out of `files`, exit code 1.
+
 Code layout: `src/count.js` does the counting, `src/cli.js` parses arguments and
 prints (`run(argv, io)` takes the IO functions as parameters so it can be
 tested without touching the real terminal or disk), `bin/tally.js` wires it to
